                                    <br><br>
                                    <h4>How to Upgrade/Install Nginx Stable=
=E2=80=AFor=E2=80=AFMainline on Ubuntu&nbsp;20.04 LTS</h4>
<p>If you=E2=80=99re running Ubuntu=E2=80=AF20.04 and need a newer nginx th=
an the one in the default repos, this guide shows two ways to get either th=
e stable or mainline build without breaking your existing sites. You=E2=80=
=99ll see why the official nginx.org repository is usually the smarter choi=
ce, and how to fall back to Ubuntu=E2=80=99s packages when you just want =
=E2=80=9Cgood enough=E2=80=9D.</p>
<h4>1. Decide which branch you actually need</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Stable</strong>&nbsp;=E2=80=93 receives only vetted bug=E2=
=80=91fixes; best for production servers that can=E2=80=99t afford surprise=
 regressions.</li>
<li><strong>Mainline</strong>&nbsp;=E2=80=93 gets the latest features (HTTP=
/3, new modules) as soon as they land; handy for testing or when a feature =
isn=E2=80=99t yet in stable.</li>
</ul>
<p>I=E2=80=99ve been burned once by pulling mainline on a box that was serv=
ing a legacy PHP app; the new TLS implementation broke compatibility with a=
n old OpenSSL library. That=E2=80=99s why I always match the branch to the =
risk level of the service.</p>
<h4>2. Method A =E2=80=93 Use Ubuntu=E2=80=99s native packages (quick but o=
utdated)</h4>
<blockquote class=3D"alert-info">sudo apt update<br>sudo apt install nginx<=
br></blockquote>
<p>Why this works: Ubuntu=E2=80=AF20.04 ships nginx=E2=80=AF1.18 from its o=
wn archive, which is a stable release that will receive security updates vi=
a the distro=E2=80=99s regular channel. If you=E2=80=99re fine with =E2=80=
=9Cgood enough=E2=80=9D and want zero extra repos, this is all you need.</p=
>
<p><strong>When not to use it:</strong> The version is frozen at 1.18.x, so=
 you won=E2=80=99t get newer directives like proxy_ssl_server_name that app=
eared in 1.19+. For most static sites it=E2=80=99s fine; for anything needi=
ng the latest HTTP/2 tweaks, look elsewhere.</p>
<h4>3. Method B =E2=80=93 Pull directly from nginx.org (recommended for fre=
sh features)</h4>
<h4>a) Add the signing key</h4>
<blockquote class=3D"alert-info">curl -fsSL <i class=3D"bi bi-box-arrow-up-=
right"></i> <a href=3D"https://nginx.org/keys/nginx_signing.key" target=3D"=
_blank" rel=3D"nofollow">https://nginx.org/keys/nginx_signing.key</a> | sud=
o gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/nginx-archive-keyring.gpg<br></block=
quote>
<p>The key guarantees that the packages really come from nginx, not some ro=
gue mirror.</p>
<h4>b) Choose stable or mainline and create a source list</h4>
<p>For <strong>stable</strong>:</p>
<blockquote class=3D"alert-info">echo "deb [signed-by=3D/usr/share/keyrings=
/nginx-archive-keyring.gpg] <i class=3D"bi bi-box-arrow-up-right"></i> <a h=
ref=3D"http://nginx.org/packages/ubuntu" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"nofollow"=
>http://nginx.org/packages/ubuntu</a> `lsb_release -cs` nginx" | sudo tee /=
etc/apt/sources.list.d/nginx-stable.list<br></blockquote>
<p>For <strong>mainline</strong>:</p>
<blockquote class=3D"alert-info">echo "deb [signed-by=3D/usr/share/keyrings=
/nginx-archive-keyring.gpg] <i class=3D"bi bi-box-arrow-up-right"></i> <a h=
ref=3D"http://nginx.org/packages/mainline/ubuntu" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"=
nofollow">http://nginx.org/packages/mainline/ubuntu</a> `lsb_release -cs` n=
ginx" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/nginx-mainline.list<br></blockquot=
e>
<p>Why separate files: It keeps the two branches isolated, so you can switc=
h later without editing a massive list.</p>
<h4>c) Update and install</h4>
<blockquote class=3D"alert-info">sudo apt update<br>sudo apt install nginx<=
br></blockquote>
<p>At this point nginx -v will report something like <strong>1.22.x</strong=
> for stable or <strong>1.23.x</strong> for mainline, depending on what you=
 chose.</p>
<p><strong>Tip:</strong> If you already have the Ubuntu package installed, =
the repo swap will automatically upgrade it in place. Your /etc/nginx/ conf=
ig directory stays untouched, but always back up nginx.conf before a major =
version jump.</p>
<h4>4. Verify the installation and restart</h4>
<blockquote class=3D"alert-info">sudo systemctl status nginx   # confirm it=
=E2=80=99s running<br>sudo nginx -t                 # syntax check your con=
fig files<br>sudo systemctl reload nginx   # apply any changes without drop=
ping connections<br></blockquote>
<p>Running nginx -V (capital V) shows the compile=E2=80=91time options. You=
=E2=80=99ll see flags like --with-http_ssl_module, which confirms you=E2=80=
=99ve got SSL support baked in.</p>
<h4>5. Rolling back if something goes sideways</h4>
<p>If the new version breaks an app, you can revert to Ubuntu=E2=80=99s pac=
kage:</p>
<blockquote class=3D"alert-info">sudo apt install nginx=3D1.18.*   # replac=
e * with the exact sub=E2=80=91version shown by apt-cache policy nginx<br><=
/blockquote>
<p>Then run sudo systemctl restart nginx. Having the original repo still en=
abled makes this painless.</p>
<h4>6. Keep it tidy</h4>
<p>Remove any leftover source list you don=E2=80=99t need:</p>
<blockquote class=3D"alert-info">sudo rm /etc/apt/sources.list.d/nginx-*.li=
st   # pick the one you=E2=80=99re not using<br>sudo apt update<br></blockq=
uote>
<p>That prevents accidental upgrades to the wrong branch later on.</p>
<p>



















</p>
<p>That=E2=80=99s it=E2=80=94pick the repo that matches your risk tolerance=
, pull the packages, and you=E2=80=99ll have a fresh nginx ready to serve.&=
nbsp;</p>
